\section{Discussion}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Alex:} The transition probabilities and matrix elements are derived under the assumption of a specific quantum system. It would be beneficial to discuss the physical implications of these expressions in the context of the system under consideration.}

The expressions for $P_D$ and $X$ are derived for a system consisting of two coupled quantum harmonic oscillators with dissipation. The transition probabilities $P_D$ and matrix elements $X$ provide insights into the dynamics of entanglement in this system. The parameter $\lambda$ represents the coupling strength between the oscillators, while $\sigma$ and $\Omega$ are related to the frequencies and damping rates of the oscillators. The parameters $\gamma_A, \gamma_B, r_h, \ell, R_A, R_B, \Delta\phi$ characterize the specific properties of the oscillators and their environment.

\textbf{\tcb{BLUE}: Robie:} It is important to note that the expressions are valid under certain approximations, such as weak coupling and low temperatures. These approximations simplify the analysis but may limit the applicability of the results to more general scenarios.

