\section{Conclusion}
\label{Concl}
The above derivation shows that the change in the local energy of a composite system can be expressed in terms of the Lindblad superoperators acting on the local density matrices and the interaction term. The result is given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:FinalEnergyChange}), which is valid for arbitrary local Hamiltonians and interaction terms. This provides a useful tool for analyzing the energy dynamics in open quantum systems. The result also highlights the role of the interaction term in the energy exchange between the subsystems. The vanishing of the superoperators on certain tensor products of identity and Hamiltonian operators indicates that the local dynamics does not affect the global energy in specific cases. This insight can be used to design quantum control strategies that maintain the energy balance in composite systems. The implications of this result for quantum thermodynamics and quantum information processing are discussed in Section~\ref{sec:Implications}.

