\subsection{II.{\quad}Decomposition of the quadratic operation}

The quadratic operation can be decomposed into a sequence of simpler operations, which are more easily implemented in an optical system. The effective Hamiltonian $\hat{H}=\kappa\hat{x}^2$ can be expressed as a product of three unitary operators:

\begin{equation}
\hat{U}(\kappa) = \hat{S}(\xi) \hat{R}(\theta) \hat{S}(\xi),
\end{equation}

where $\hat{S}(\xi)$ is the squeezing operator with the squeezing parameter $\xi = \frac{1}{2}\ln(1+\kappa^2)$, and $\hat{R}(\theta)$ is the phase shift operator with the phase shift $\theta = \arctan\kappa$. The squeezing operator $\hat{S}(\xi)$ is implemented by a nonlinear optical process such as optical parametric amplification, and the phase shift operator $\hat{R}(\theta)$ is implemented by a phase modulator. The sequence of these operations realizes the desired quadratic operation.

