\paragraph{Conclusion.}
The error bound in Eq.~\eqref{eq:error1} thus simplifies to
\begin{equation}
\epsilon_{\rho,\sigma} \leq 3 \Vert A \Vert_{\max}^2.
\end{equ{equation}
This result shows that the error of our method is controlled by the square of the maximum absolute element of the matrix $A$. This is a significant improvement over the naive error estimate of $\mathcal{O}(N^2)$, which would result from a direct application of the triangle inequality without exploiting the structure of the swap matrix. The bound is tight for matrices with a single large element and small or zero elements elsewhere. In practice, the error can be further reduced by using a preconditioning step to reduce the maximum absolute element of $A$. This preconditioning step can be implemented efficiently using quantum algorithms, as shown in Ref.~\cite{Low2016}. In summary, our method provides a practical and efficient way to implement quantum algorithms for solving linear systems, with error bounds that are controllable and can be made arbitrarily small by appropriate choice of the matrix $A$.

