The experimental setup for the generation and detection of the OAM entangled states is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:setup}. A 405 nm diode laser is used to pump a type-II $\beta$-barium borate (BBO) crystal to generate photon pairs via spontaneous parametric down-conversion (SPDC). The pump beam is focused into the BBO crystal using a lens with a focal length of 100 mm. The generated photon pairs are separated by a polarizing beam splitter (PBS) and sent to two single-mode fibers (SMFs) with different OAM modes. The OAM states are generated by passing the photons through a spatial light modulator (SLM) with a phase pattern corresponding to the desired OAM state. The SLMs are controlled by a computer to generate the desired OAM states. The photons are then detected by single-photon avalanche photodiodes (SPADs) with a detection efficiency of 60\%.

