\subsection{Matching conditions}
The matching of the interior metric (\ref{3}) to the exterior E--R metric
requires the continuity of the first and second fundamental forms across
the boundary surface $\Sigma$. The first fundamental form is just the
metric itself, while the second fundamental form is related to the
extrinsic curvature. The Darmois conditions \cite{Darmois} state that
these two forms must be continuous across the boundary surface. The
interior metric (\ref{3}) is matched to the exterior E--R metric
\begin{equation}
ds^2_+=-dt^2+dr^2+dz^2+e^{2\sigma(r)}d\phi^2, \label{4}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(r)$ is a function of $r$ only. The continuity of the first
fundamental form gives the matching conditions
\begin{equation}
A^2(t,r)=1, \quad B^2(t,r)=1, \quad C^2(t,r)=e^{2\sigma(r)}. \label{5}
\end{equation}

