\subsection{Zemach Formalism}
\subsubsection{Introduction}
The Zemach formalism is a powerful tool to describe reactions involving multiple particles with different spins. It provides a systematic way to handle the angular momentum couplings in a reaction. The formalism is based on the use of symmetric and traceless tensors to represent the angular momentum states of the particles involved. This approach simplifies the analysis of complex reactions by breaking them down into manageable components.

\subsubsection{Symmetric and Traceless Tensors}
In the Zemach formalism, the angular momentum states of particles are represented by symmetric and traceless tensors. A tensor of rank $l$ is symmetric if its components remain unchanged under any permutation of its indices. It is traceless if the sum of its diagonal components is zero. These properties ensure that the tensors can correctly represent the angular momentum states without introducing unphysical degrees of freedom.

