\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{subsection:problem_formulation}

The problem of fMRI imputation can be formulated as a multivariate time series imputation problem. Given an incomplete fMRI instance $\psi \in \mathbb{R}^{v \times t} \cup \{nan\}$ and its mask $\mu \in \{0,1\}^{v \times t}$, the goal is to impute the missing values, $\iota \in \mathbb{R}^{v \times t}$, such that $\psi + \iota = \varphi$, where $\varphi$ is the complete fMRI instance. The imputation model $I$ is trained to minimize a loss function $L(\psi, \iota, \varphi)$, which measures the difference between the imputed values $\iota$ and the ground truth values $\varphi$. The loss function is defined as:

