\section{Spin wave fluctuations around canted N\'eel state}

The Hamiltonian, with a magnetic field and second neighbour exchange, is
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{Eq:Hnnexch}
H = J_1 \sum_{\langle i j\rangle} {\bf S}_i \cdot {\bf S}_j + J_2  \sum_{\langle\la i j \rangle\ra} {\bf S}_i \cdot {\bf S}_j  - B
\sum_i S_{i}^{z}.
\end{eqnarray}
As discussed in the main body, when $J_2 < J_1/6$ and $B < 6J_1 S$, N\'eel ordering is in the plane perpendicular to the magnetic field, but
the spins also uniformly cant in the direction of applied field, to maximally gain Zeeman energy. The classical spin state can be characterized by ${\bf S}_{{\bf{r}}} \!=\! S (\pm \cos\chi,0,\sin\chi)$ on the two sublattices. We now define new spin operators, denoted by ${\mathbf T}_{i,\alpha}$, via a sublattice-dependent local spin rotation 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
       T_{i,\alpha}^x \\ T_{i,\alpha}^y \\ T_{i,\alpha}^z
      \end{array} \right)\!\! =\!\!
\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
       \sin\chi & 0 & (-)^{\alpha+1}\cos\chi \\
	0 & 1 & 0 \\
	(-)^\alpha \cos\chi & 0 & \sin\chi \end{array}\right)\!\!
\left(\begin{array}{c}
       S_{i,\alpha}^x \\ S_{i,\alpha}^y \\ S_{i,\alpha}^z
      \end{array} \right),
\end{eqnarray}
where $\alpha=1,2$, is a sublattice index and $i$ sums over each unit cell. The ground state has all spins pointing towards the new local-$S^z$ axis. To study spin wave fluctuations, we rewrite the 
$T$ operators in terms of Holstein-Primakoff bosons as follows:
\begin{eqnarray}
\nonumber T_{i,\alpha}^{z}\!\! &=& \! S-b_{i,\alpha}^{\dagger} b_{i,\alpha}, \\
\nonumber T_{i,\alpha}^{x}\!\! &=& \!\sqrt{\frac{S}{2}}(b_{i,\alpha}+b_{i,\alpha}^{\dagger}), \\
\nonumber T_{i,\alpha}^{y}\!\! &=& \!\frac{1}{i}\sqrt{\frac{S}{2}}(b_{i,\alpha}-b_{i,\alpha}^{\dagger}). 
\end{eqnarray}

The Hamiltonian can now be rewritten as $H\approx E_{Cl}+ H_{qu}$. The classical energy $E_{Cl}$ is proportional to $S^2$, and the leading order quantum correction, $H_{qu}$, is of order $S$. We get the value of the canting angle $\chi$ by demanding that terms of order $S^{3/2}$, which are linear in the boson operators, should vanish, which yields
\begin{eqnarray}
\sin\!\chi = \frac{B}{6J_1 S}.
\end{eqnarray}
The classical energy is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{E_{Cl} }{N S^2}=-\frac{3}{2}J_1 \cos2\chi + \frac{3}{2}J_2 - \frac{B}{S} \sin\!\chi.
\end{eqnarray}
where $N$ is the number of sites in the honeycomb lattice. We take the magnetic field $B$ to be of order $S$, so that the Zeeman 
term $-BS_{i}^{z}$ is treated on the same level as the exchange terms $J_{ij} {\bf S}_{i}\!\cdot\!{\bf S}_{j}$. The leading quantum correction
\begin{eqnarray}
